    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "For this study, a set of normal faults exposed in Hanksville, UT that offset the Jurassic Entrada and Curtis Formations are analyzed. The faults and stratigraphy were mapped on photo mosaics of a kilometer-long cliff face. Field data suggests that soft units such as mudstone or siltstone may influence the development and geometry of conjugate normal faults. Finite element models are used to investigate how stiffness of sedimentary layers affects the perturbed stress fields around faults. Boundary element models are used to assess how the intersection angle between conjugate normal faults affects their mechanical interaction. Results from the numerical models indicate that conjugate geometry and soft layers near fault intersections may have a strong effect on the growth and development of conjugate faults.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
